# Authorization on Coolify: application "authz", branch authz-backend-production, Dockerfile build, port 8104, network alias authz, NO domain.
# Connect authz directly to the existing private PostgreSQL resource. Paste these variables into authz's Developer View, replacing every <...>.
# Keep generated secrets in Coolify; never commit a filled copy of this file.
# HTTP port; default 8104. Match Coolify's container port and the callers' AUTHZ_URL.
PORT=8104
# Secret PostgreSQL URI of the existing private authz database. Include sslmode=require. The service creates its own authz_* tables.
DATABASE_URL=<postgres://user:password@private-postgres:5432/database?sslmode=require>
# Generate once: PowerShell `[Convert]::ToBase64String([Security.Cryptography.RandomNumberGenerator]::GetBytes(32))` (a 32-byte Ed25519 seed).
# Or run `go run . new-key` from services/authz. Paste the output here and keep it stable across redeploys.
# Secret Ed25519 key (base64 PKCS#8 or 32-byte seed) that signs grants; `service new-key` prints one. Keep stable; empty uses a temporary key.
AUTHZ_SIGNING_KEY=<paste a new-key output>
# One secret per calling service. If Coolify already has billing=<existing token>, keep it unchanged and append ,auth=<new token> to this SAME variable.
# Do not add a separate AUTHZ_TOKEN variable to authz. Generate the new token in PowerShell:
#   $bytes = New-Object byte[] 32; [Security.Cryptography.RandomNumberGenerator]::Create().GetBytes($bytes); -join ($bytes | ForEach-Object { $_.ToString('x2') })
# Copy only the 64-character result after auth=. It must differ from billing's token; authz refuses duplicate tokens.
# Then put that same result (without the auth= prefix) in the auth application's AUTHZ_TOKEN. Save and redeploy authz before auth.
# Secret caller tokens, caller=token comma-separated, 32+ characters each; auth reads account memberships, billing updates standing. Each caller gets only its own rights.
AUTHZ_TOKENS=billing=<billing token>,auth=<auth token>
# How long a grant lasts, 5 to 600 seconds; default 60. Shorter means faster revocation and more calls.
AUTHZ_GRANT_SECONDS=60
# Default true: roles that need the second step (owner, solo) must have passed it. false only for local testing.
AUTHZ_REQUIRE_SECOND_STEP=true
# Default true: Caller Whale's staff must have passed the second step. Keep true in production.
AUTHZ_STAFF_SECOND_STEP=true
# To set the first Caller Whale owner, sign in once, then look up the Caller Whale Account ID in the auth database by Firebase UID.
# In psql on Caller Whale Auth PostgreSQL: SELECT id FROM users WHERE firebase_uid = 'the-actual-firebase-uid';
# The same id is /v1/me.id and the sign-in token's sub. Email addresses and Firebase IDs do not work.
# If you have not signed up yet, leave this empty, deploy authz, then set the ID and redeploy. It only fills an empty owner slot.
# Optional. Caller Whale auth database users.id UUID (never the Firebase UID). Applied at start only when Caller Whale has no owner yet; it never replaces one.
AUTHZ_PLATFORM_OWNER=
# Optional. Set once to the cofounder's Account ID (UUID). The first value is permanent across redeploys; changing this variable cannot replace them.
AUTHZ_PLATFORM_COFOUNDER=
